## Assessment by question

**1. Section 0.** The probe results are restated accurately, with the r1 scoping incorporated ("this host's JVM", probe date, host-escalation distinction). The discipline is the right core and is neither too soft nor preachy. But the scorecard line "You have three of the four" is itself an unverified claim about the harness — §5.2's own trap. The fourth capability, "shell out to the delegate CLIs", was never probed: the only observed-allowed exec is `/usr/bin/shasum` (system path) and the only blocked one is `~/.local/share/mise/.../java` (home path), while *every* delegate CLI on this host is home-local (`claude`, `agy`, `glm`, `deepseek`, `muse` in `~/.local/bin`; `codex` in `~/.nvm`). If that path class is what the sandbox denies, the seat cannot route at all, and §0's remedy "route the run to a delegate" collapses to "ask the user" — one read-only command would settle it. Second gap: §0 scopes the block to "test suites", but every `clojure` invocation is equally dead (the cljd compile gate, shadow-cljs builds, `:cljd-yin-repl-build`, the `:kondo` alias), so the R5 "full cljd namespace compilation gates each phase" check is also unavailable.

**2. Factual accuracy.** The author's own verified items all check out, as does nearly everything else — with two flat falsehoods. First, "Working tree has no uncommitted tracked changes" is false: three *staged deletions* sit in the index (`docs/datomworld.demo.artifact_scene.md`, `docs/orchestrator-handoff.md`, `docs/postgraphics.refactor.md`), and §3 "Live state" covers lesser items while omitting these. Combined with the "commit only staged changes" convention, a successor authorized to commit could silently sweep the deletions in. Second, "No commit in this repository's history carries a trailer" is false: 12 commits carry `Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>`, 6 of them in this branch's history, the newest from 2026-08-27 — a week before this document. Both are exactly the class of error the document catalogues in §5. Minor: "Eight commits since the previous handover (`6422c8b`)" — the surviving previous handoff doc is dated 2026-09-02 18:43 GMT, four commits before `6422c8b` (2026-09-04 08:50 GMT); if "previous handover" means the undocumented glm→claude-opus-5 rotation at `6422c8b`, say so.

**3. Section 4's independence warning.** Correct per TEAM.md's family rule, and the "remains useful to read" nuance is right. Incomplete in two small ways: `0466149a` is also AGY/Gemini and is only implicitly covered, and the "route to `glm-5.3`, `claude-*`, or `qwen`" list reads as exhaustive while omitting `gpt-5.6-sol`, the Routine Review primary.

**4. Usefulness.** Strong. A cold orchestrator gets exact state, the defining two-process constraint with the plan's own reasoning quoted, concrete fact lists, verified entry points, resumable sessions with IDs, and traps that are all specific. The sequencing *is* argued (misattribution rationale) and correctly flagged as unruled. Filler is minimal. The important absences are the staged deletions and the delegate-CLI probe above; also worth one clause: the handoff's "five separable facts" silently corrects the plan's own miscount (`dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:501` says "four", then lists five) — a successor cross-checking will trip on the discrepancy.

**5. Overstatement.** The suite table, the `6422c8b` end-condition caveat, and the sequencing are properly hedged. Not hedged: the working-tree claim, the trailer-history claim, and "routing … fully available to you". Small note: §0's present-tense "cannot execute this host's JVM" and unattributed `BypassSandbox` mechanism are marginally stronger than TEAM.md's committed "could not … when probed 2026-09-04 … by AGY's own account".

